Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.005 --> 00:00:01.380
SPEAKER: Google
Kubernetes Engine

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.420
helps you focus on
your application

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.310
while still being able
to configure details

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.110
of how your app can run.

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:08.910
When it comes to
cost optimization,

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.820
you can fine-tune both your
application and your GKE

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.850
configuration, getting the most
value for what you're spending

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:16.980
and reducing wasted resources.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.260
Today we'll talk about some
tips and best practices

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.270
for the application
side of that.

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:23.507
Let's go beyond your GKE bill.

00:00:23.507 --> 00:00:25.495
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.030
In the past few
videos, we've spent

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:35.090
a lot of time looking
at optimizing GKE

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:38.820
through monitoring, machine
types, autoscaling, and more.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.760
If you haven't watched
the previous videos,

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:42.710
I'd suggest checking
those out first,

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:45.920
since a lot of specific
optimization tips in this video

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.670
and the next builds on
those previous concepts.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:51.540
We're talking all
about apps today.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.600
So I'll start with
the resource side,

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.640
then move on to some
best practices for how

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.050
to build your containers.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:58.640
After that, we'll
talk about some ways

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:00.620
to make sure that your
app and Kubernetes are

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:02.690
working well together.

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:05.110
The best way to start
is to repeat this.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:08.230
It is critical to know the
details of your application

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:10.390
and how it responds to demand.

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:12.370
Monitoring can help
understand what resources

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:14.950
are used as demand increases.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.280
We covered monitoring earlier,
but here's another tip.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.400
Try running your application
as a single pod replica

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:24.670
and then test it under load
without any autoscaling.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:26.230
Once you have that
information, use

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:28.480
it to configure resource
requests and limits, which

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.040
we'll talk about in a minute.

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.830
Then test your app again
with cluster autoscaling

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:35.590
and horizontal or
vertical autoscaling.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:38.410
Now test large, rapid
spikes in demand

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:40.180
to see how your app responds.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.850
No matter which tool you
use to stimulate demand,

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:45.100
try to use the
same region or zone

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:46.600
as where your app is running.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:48.430
That way you can
minimize latency

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:51.010
and focus on understanding
all the details of how

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:52.570
your app scales.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:54.730
And there's more you can
do with that knowledge.

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:56.290
Earlier in the
monitoring video, we

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:58.600
talked about resource
quotas and limit ranges.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.310
You can also set
resource requests

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:03.010
and use limit ranges
to control resources.

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:04.870
With a resource
request, you're defining

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:06.940
how many resources
your application

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:08.710
actually needs to run.

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:10.330
With a resource
limit, you're defining

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:12.205
the maximum amount of
resources that your app

00:02:12.205 --> 00:02:13.468
is allowed to use.

00:02:13.468 --> 00:02:15.010
These will help
Kubernetes figure out

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:16.900
how to schedule
your pods and ensure

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:20.440
that they don't use up all the
resources that are on the node.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.360
One best practice is
to set your memory

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.670
limit equal to
your memory request

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:28.240
and to set your CPU limit
higher than your CPU request.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.280
Or you can even
leave it unbounded.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.140
Kubernetes can always
throttle the CPU

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:34.630
that your pods are using
down to the request amount

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.330
if the CPU resources
are in high demand.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.400
But the same doesn't
work for memory,

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.610
since Kubernetes needs to
shut down pods when scaling

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:45.530
and if there's too much
demand on the memory.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:47.300
Of course, any
form of autoscaling

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:50.840
might not be very useful if
your application can't scale.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.820
Vertical autoscaling,
it's only effective

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:55.610
if your application can actually
use the additional resources

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:57.080
on the nodes.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.180
In the same way,
horizontal autoscaling

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:02.450
is only effective if additional
pod replicas actually let

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:04.460
your app handle more demand.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:05.990
If the actual
performance bottleneck

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:08.240
is on something like
a required database,

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.450
more pods might actually
make the problem worse.

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:13.610
Either way, you can use
the different autoscaling

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:16.040
techniques that we went
over in the previous videos

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:18.800
to make a configuration that
works best for your app.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:20.912
But if your app can't
scale, you might

00:03:20.912 --> 00:03:23.370
need to consider refactoring
to a structure that works well

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.180
with Kubernetes.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.520
For example,
single-threaded apps or apps

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:30.820
that can only run a limited
number of concurrent processes

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:33.570
may not get much value out
of the extra resources.

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.770
If your app can
scale, the next step

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:38.810
is to look at spinning
up and spinning down.

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:42.350
Here's some best practices
for minimizing startup times.

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:44.810
Whenever the cluster
autoscaler creates a new node,

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:47.450
that node has to download
the base image for each app

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:48.440
that it'll run.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:52.170
The smaller your image is, the
faster a node can download it.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.600
In the same way, use
the smallest base image

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.820
that your app
actually needs to run.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.340
Also try to minimize the
amount of startup time needed

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:01.140
to start handling requests.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.900
Startup tasks like
loading classes and caches

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:07.110
can significantly increase the
amount of time between creation

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.900
and then being ready, which
could cause requests to fail

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:11.760
while your app is spinning up.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.673
Check out the best
practices for building

00:04:13.673 --> 00:04:15.090
containers link
in the description

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:17.352
below for more information.

00:04:17.352 --> 00:04:19.019
On the other side,
you want to make sure

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:21.060
that your app can
shut down gracefully.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.930
If your workload has
trouble shutting down,

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:25.860
it can lead to delays and
disruptions in service.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:27.580
When a pod is going
to be shut down,

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:29.160
it should receive a SIGTERM.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.320
Instead of just assuming
that everything is done,

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.870
this is actually a signal
to do some cleanup.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.900
First of all, you want to update
your readiness probe to fail.

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:38.440
More on that coming soon.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:40.590
So that request will
stop coming into the pod

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:43.500
if you're using container native
load balancing, which we'll

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.430
talk about in the next video.

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:48.900
But your pod must still
finish all the active requests

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.960
and any new ones that come in,
since it can take a bit of time

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:54.360
for Kubernetes to update
where those requests are

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.950
going to be delivered to.

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:58.770
In addition to that, your pod
should run any cleanup steps

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:01.470
like persisting any
in-memory state.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:03.228
If you're not handling
SIGTERM or you

00:05:03.228 --> 00:05:05.520
can't because your app is
using a system that you don't

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.620
actually have control
over, like a web

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.790
server or a reverse proxy,
you can use the preStop hook.

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:13.830
Here your pod can
run the same cleanup,

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:16.590
and you can even put a sleep
command in for a few seconds

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:18.597
to postpone the SIGTERM.

00:05:18.597 --> 00:05:21.180
There's also a configuration to
give your pods a bit more time

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:22.080
to shut down.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.450
It's called
terminationGracePeriodSeconds.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:27.630
You should only change
this if you really need it.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.700
By default, it's
set to 30 seconds,

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:32.380
but it can go all the way up to
10 minutes before the cluster

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:35.130
autoscaler forcibly
terminates it.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:36.930
Quick startups and
proper shutdowns

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:39.960
help your autoscalers work in a
more reliable, more efficient,

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:43.050
and cost optimized way.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.840
The more specific you configure
GKE to your application,

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:47.160
the better it can run it.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:50.100
In the first video, we talked
about pod disruption budgets,

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:52.800
when you statically set the
minimum number of pad replicas

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.200
to keep your app running.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:55.950
Kubernetes will try
to respect that number

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.920
as it handles rollouts,
upgrades, and autoscaling.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:01.500
It's not a guarantee when
unexpected things happen,

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:03.990
but it's still a best practice
to define pod disruption

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:06.150
budgets for every application.

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.010
That way your app
will almost always

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:11.160
have enough replicas to
keep running effectively.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:12.660
In addition to those
budgets, you'll

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:14.493
also want to make sure
that you're correctly

00:06:14.493 --> 00:06:17.220
setting readiness and
liveness probes for your app.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:19.020
Kubernetes will use
the readiness probe

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:21.540
to know if each pod is
ready to handle traffic.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:24.240
So you don't need to worry about
race conditions between traffic

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:25.910
and setup time.

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:27.980
Liveness probes are used
to tell if the pod is

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:29.610
still alive and working.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:31.850
So if your application
gets stuck or deadlocked,

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:33.890
Kubernetes knows to restart it.

00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:36.410
These two probes are key to
making sure that Kubernetes can

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:38.220
optimize working with your app.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:41.450
And here are some best
practices for working with them.

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:44.870
Always define readiness
probes for every container.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:47.180
Know when your application
is actually ready.

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:50.210
For example, if your app
depends on a cache being loaded,

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:51.800
the readiness probe
should only say

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:55.040
the pod is ready after
that cache has been loaded.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:57.350
If your app doesn't
need any startup time,

00:06:57.350 --> 00:06:59.840
define a simple readiness
probe, such as just returning

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:01.840
HTTP 200.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:04.030
Also make sure the
code for your probes

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:06.340
is as simple and
isolated as possible

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:08.140
so there's no blockers
when Kubernetes

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:09.970
is requesting the status.

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:12.940
Your probe logic should try
to avoid remote services.

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:14.900
And if you have to
use something remote,

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:19.100
try to test against the simplest
and hopefully fastest one.

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:21.620
One more best practice for
any and all of your services

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:24.140
is to consider how
to handle retries.

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:26.270
Especially in a full
microservices environment,

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:28.730
a service call could fail
for any number of reasons,

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:31.190
like a network failure
or rate limits.

00:07:31.190 --> 00:07:32.720
When there's a
failure, it usually

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:34.980
makes sense to retry
the request in case

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:36.630
it was something temporary.

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:38.660
But if it's more
permanent, you may end up

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:40.880
sending lots of requests
and potentially even

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:43.040
overwhelming the destination.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:44.780
For both the way
you call services

00:07:44.780 --> 00:07:46.940
and the way you create
your own services,

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:49.980
use exponential backoff to
protect against overwhelming

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:51.020
the service.

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:52.790
In short, you just
add a bit of time

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:55.760
after the first failure and then
more if there's another failure

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:57.680
and continue exponentially.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:00.530
When calling a service, you can
implement exponential backoff

00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:04.460
logic yourself, use a library,
or even use Istio or Anthos

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:07.280
Service Mesh that support
a proxy level retry which

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:08.793
handles it all for you.

00:08:08.793 --> 00:08:10.460
If your service is
the one being called,

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:12.830
you can rate limit requests
from the same caller

00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:14.930
to force exponential backoff.

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:16.610
You'll also want
to prepare yourself

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:19.940
for handling retry requests so
you don't end up duplicating

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:21.740
functionality in a bad way.

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:23.840
Bank transactions, for example.

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:26.420
Of course, the specific details
of how to implement that

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:28.380
will depend on your application.

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:30.130
These are some things
to keep in mind when

00:08:30.130 --> 00:08:32.505
working with your application,
and each one will give you

00:08:32.505 --> 00:08:33.620
different results.

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:36.880
The key here is to really
have your application and GKE

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:39.549
work together as
efficiently as possible.

00:08:39.549 --> 00:08:42.400
On the next video, we'll look
at tips for optimizing GKE

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:43.538
and do a wrap-up.

00:08:43.538 --> 00:08:45.580
Remember to check out the
link in the description

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:48.700
for the full guide on
GKE cost optimization.

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:51.450
